[cot:thought] Planning a post on how multilateral institutions allocate voting power and authority.
[cot:thought] Evaluating three angles:
[cot:thought] IMF quota shares versus world GDP, World Bank voting power concentration, and veto points with executive constraints.
[cot:thought] Settled on the "Multilateral
[cot:thought] voting power vs economic weight" angle. The post will contrast formal authority in Bretton Woods institutions, the UN Security Council, and related bodies against GDP and population.
[cot:thought] Key data sources include IMF quota shares, World Bank IBRD voting, UNSC veto versus UNGA one-vote, historical quota reforms, and SDR allocations.
[cot:thought] Drafting a headline
[cot:thought] comparing US and Chinese IMF quota shares. Checking exact figures from the 16th General Review of Quotas. The 50% quota increase was equiproportional.
[cot:thought] Relative shares remain unchanged. Executive Directors chairs represent 190 members across 24 seats.
[cot:thought] Exact IMF quota shares
[cot:thought] are now set: US 17.43%, China 6.41%, Japan 6.48%, Germany 5.59%, France 4.23%, UK 4.23%, Italy 3.16%, India 2.76%, Russia 2.71%, Brazil 2.32%. The 85% special majority threshold means the US can block major decisions alone.
[cot:thought] World Bank IBRD shares are US ~15.9%, Japan ~7.0%, China ~5.9%. A cross-country executive constraint metric will broaden the analysis beyond Bretton Woods institutions. The dashboard layout will avoid the KPI-plus-bar template.
[cot:thought] Six dashboard panels
[cot:thought] are planned. A hierarchy treemap will map authority layers from UNSC P5 through IMF Board to UNGA. A scatter plot will compare vote share against GDP share.
[cot:thought] A stacked timeline will show quota reform history. Diverging bars will highlight over- and under-representation.
[cot:thought] A radar or heat map will score multi-institution authority by country. Controls will toggle between IMF, IBRD, and UNSC, filter by region, and switch metrics.
